    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d213">

                <pb facs="0332" n="252" sameAs="frus:frus1936v01/pg252"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 Mutual Guarantee (Locarno)/460:
                    Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Yugoslavia</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Wilson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Belgrade</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1936-03-17T10:00:00+01:00">March 17, 1936—10
                            a.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 11:30 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>11. In conversation with Prime Minister he stated that while appreciating extreme
                    gravity of European situation he is optimistic of eventual peaceful solution.
                    Nevertheless in case of failure Yugoslavia like other Little and Balkan Entente
                    countries must give France full and unqualified support. Yugoslav economic life
                    has been seriously disrupted by sanctions against Italy which were adopted most
                    reluctantly and Government is therefore greatly alarmed and opposed to
                    possibility of being forced to adopt similar action against Germany its next
                    most important customer with which its profitable commercial relations have been
                    showing constant improvement.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Wilson</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
